# Target C — physical-boundary two-arc transport

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Status:** proof-only research candidate; theorem state unchanged  
**Level I / C-II:** open

This note sharpens the proof-only Section 116 two-arc transport argument at the
level of an edge-connected strict-rhombus component. Instead of routing train ends
through the outer contact walk plus all nonquadrilateral-face cycles, it uses only the
**actual physical boundary of the rhombus component**.

The finite inequality is

\[
\boxed{
 \rho(P-2\rho)
 \ge
 \sum_{R\in\mathcal R}
 \left(4-4\cos{\varphi_R\over2}\right)\operatorname{area}(R)
 > (4-2\sqrt3)A.
}
\tag{PB.1}
\]

Here `mathcal R` is one finite edge-connected component of strict unit-rhombus
contact faces, `P` is its physical boundary-edge occurrence count including hole
boundaries, `rho` is the Euclidean diameter of its vertex set, `A` is its total
rhombus area, and `varphi_R in (pi/3,pi/2]` is the acute projective angle of `R`.

The theorem also gives a globally injective version for the full strict-rhombus
complex. It therefore resolves the *multiplicity* issue in proof-only Section 120 if
one is willing to change the left-hand currency from the sum of harmonic-cell detours
to actual physical rhombus boundary. A hostile comparison at the end shows that the
resulting Type-II scalar is not yet a closure: the existing near-disk outer-boundary
lower bound is stronger at leading order.

No source point is deleted, moved, or reclassified.

---

## 1. Physical boundary and train preliminaries

Let `mathcal R` be a nonempty finite edge-connected collection of bounded strict
unit-rhombus faces in a straight-line plane contact graph. Two rhombi belong to the
same component when they can be joined through shared full rhombus edges.

Let

- `P` be the number of physical boundary-edge occurrences of the union of the
  rhombi, counting the outer boundary walk and every bounded hole boundary walk;
- `A=sum_R area(R)`;
- `rho=diam V(mathcal R)`.

Canonical Section 89 records the elementary local train facts needed below:

1. every local train has exactly two physical boundary ends, holes included;
2. every physical boundary-edge occurrence is the end of exactly one local train;
3. if `tau` has projective unit edge direction `u_tau`, then
   \[
   A_\tau:=\sum_{R\in\tau}\operatorname{area}(R)
   \]
   is exactly the transverse displacement between the midpoints of its two terminal
   edges.

Both terminal midpoints lie in `conv V(mathcal R)`, hence

\[
\boxed{A_\tau\le\rho.}
\tag{PB.2}

No simply-connectedness assumption is used.

---

## 2. Cut the physical outer boundary at a component diameter

Choose component vertices `p_-,p_+` with

\[
|p_+-p_-|=\rho.
\]

A diameter endpoint of a finite point set is extreme in its convex hull: if `p_-`
were a nontrivial convex combination of other component vertices, strict convexity of
squared distance to `p_+` would contradict maximality. Thus both endpoints occur on
the unbounded-face boundary of the rhombus union.

Choose outer-boundary occurrences of them and cut the oriented outer boundary walk at
those occurrences. The two resulting directed unit-edge routes have displacement
vectors

\[
z:=p_+-p_-,\qquad -z.
\]

Put `q=z/rho`. Orient every bounded hole boundary so that the rhombic region stays on
its left. Every hole route is closed and has displacement zero. Thus all physical
boundary occurrences are partitioned into routes with hosts

\[
\boxed{q,-q,0.}
\tag{PB.3}

The two open routes have total chord length `2rho`, while the closed routes have chord
length zero. Their total route excess is therefore exactly

\[
\boxed{E=P-2\rho.}
\tag{PB.4}

Repeated boundary vertices and pinches are harmless because this is boundary-
**occurrence** bookkeeping.

---

## 3. Signed return on physical train ends

For a train direction `u`, define

\[
\delta_q(u):=2-2|q\cdot u|.
\tag{PB.5}

For one directed unit-edge route with edge vectors `z_1,...,z_m`, displacement `z`,
and host `q_z=z/|z|` when `z!=0` (or `q_z=0` when `z=0`), projection gives

\[
\sum_{j=1}^m(1-q_z\cdot z_j)=m-|z|.
\tag{PB.6}

The two end-edge orientations of a train are opposite. If its two ends are assigned
hosts `q_1,q_2 in {q,-q,0}`, their combined contribution to (PB.6) is

\[
2-u\cdot(q_1-q_2)
\]

for one choice of sign of the projective direction `u`. Since

\[
|u\cdot(q_1-q_2)|\le2|q\cdot u|,
\]

the contribution is at least `delta_q(u)`. Every physical boundary occurrence is used
once, so

\[
\boxed{E\ge\sum_\tau\delta_q(u_\tau).}
\tag{PB.7}

---

## 4. Exact local two-direction cost

Fix one strict rhombus `R`. Let `u,v` be its two projective train directions and
`varphi in (pi/3,pi/2]` their acute projective angle. For every unit vector `q`,

\[
|q\cdot u|+|q\cdot v|\le2\cos(\varphi/2).
\tag{PB.8}

Indeed choose signs of `u,v` making their scalar products with `q` nonnegative. The
angle between those representatives is either `varphi` or `pi-varphi`, the latter no
smaller; the norm of their sum is therefore at most `2cos(varphi/2)`.

Consequently

\[
\boxed{
\delta_q(u)+\delta_q(v)
\ge4-4\cos(\varphi/2).
}
\tag{PB.9}

Triangle-freeness gives `varphi>pi/3`, so

\[
4-4\cos(\varphi/2)>4-2\sqrt3.
\tag{PB.10}

---

## 5. Area transport proves the component theorem

Multiply (PB.9) by `area(R)` and sum over rhombi. Each rhombus belongs to exactly two
trains, hence

\[
\begin{aligned}
&\sum_{R\in\mathcal R}
 \left(4-4\cos{\varphi_R\over2}\right)\operatorname{area}(R)\\
&\qquad\le
\sum_\tau\delta_q(u_\tau)
\sum_{R\in\tau}\operatorname{area}(R)
=\sum_\tau\delta_q(u_\tau)A_\tau.
\end{aligned}
\tag{PB.11}

Use (PB.2), (PB.7), and (PB.4):

\[
\sum_\tau\delta_q(u_\tau)A_\tau
\le\rho\sum_\tau\delta_q(u_\tau)
\le\rho E
=\rho(P-2\rho).
\tag{PB.12}

This proves (PB.1).

---

## 6. Exact disconnected and global forms

Let an arbitrary finite strict-rhombus complex have edge-connected components `Q_i`,
with physical perimeters `P_i`, component diameters `rho_i`, and areas `A_i`. Suppose
the whole complex lies in a convex set of diameter `d`. Put

\[
P_R:=\sum_iP_i,\qquad A_4:=\sum_iA_i.
\]

Each `P_i-2rho_i` is nonnegative and `rho_i<=d`. Summing (PB.1) gives

\[
\boxed{
 d\left(P_R-2\sum_i\rho_i\right)
 \ge
 \sum_R\left(4-4\cos{\varphi_R\over2}\right)\operatorname{area}(R)
 > (4-2\sqrt3)A_4.
}
\tag{PB.13}

Now apply this to the **full strict-rhombus complex of the canonical positive-contact
core**. Its convex ambient force hull has diameter exactly the full source diameter
`D`, so `d=D`. The physical boundary is globally occurrence-injective. Every physical
rhombus-boundary edge has on its other side either the outer contact face or one
bounded nonquadrilateral contact face. Therefore, with canonical global notation,

\[
P_R\le b+(4f+Q).
\tag{PB.14}

The exact post-ZF face ledger is

\[
b+Q=2(h-s_Z).
\tag{PB.15}

Combining (PB.13)--(PB.15) yields the finite source-level currency

\[
\boxed{
 h-s_Z+2f-\sum_i\rho_i
 > (2-\sqrt3){A_4\over D}.
}
\tag{PB.16}

This is the globally injective counterpart that the harmonic-cell sum in proof-only
Section 120 does not provide. No physical boundary occurrence can pay twice in
(PB.16).

---

## 7. Area saturation forces the component spans to cover a diameter projection

The subtraction `sum rho_i` in (PB.16) is not disposable. In the Type-II branch it
has a useful lower bound.

### Lemma PB.1 — projection coverage

Let `K_D` be convex planar bodies with

\[
\operatorname{diam}(K_D)=d_D\le D,
\qquad
\operatorname{area}(K_D)\ge cD^2
\]

for fixed `c>0`. Let `U_D subset K_D` be a union of connected compact pieces
`U_{D,i}` with

\[
\operatorname{area}(K_D\setminus U_D)=o(D^2).
\]

If `rho_{D,i}=diam(U_{D,i})`, then

\[
\boxed{\sum_i\rho_{D,i}\ge d_D-o(D).}
\tag{PB.17}

#### Proof

Project onto a diameter direction and translate the projection interval to `[0,d_D]`.
Let `f(x)` be the perpendicular section length. It is concave. If `H=max f`, then
`H>=area(K_D)/d_D>=cD`.

Let `M` be the set of projection coordinates missed by `U_D`, with measure `m`, and
choose `x_0` with `f(x_0)=H`. Concavity gives tent lower bounds on both sides of
`x_0`. If `M` has measures `m_-` and `m_+` on those sides, the smallest possible
integrals of the tent bounds give

\[
\int_Mf(x)\,dx
\ge {H\over2D}(m_-^2+m_+^2)
\ge {H\over4D}m^2
\ge {c\over4}m^2.
\]

The left side is at most `area(K_D\setminus U_D)=o(D^2)`, so `m=o(D)`.
Each connected piece has an interval as projection, whose length is at most its
diameter. Hence

\[
\sum_i\rho_{D,i}
\ge |proj(U_D)|
=d_D-m=d_D-o(D).
\]

`\square`

In Type II, take `K_D=F` and `U_D` to be the union of all strict rhombi. Canonical
Section 80 gives

\[
area(F)/D^2\to\pi/4,
\qquad
A_4=area(F)-o(D^2),
\]

and `diam(F)=D`. Thus

\[
\boxed{\sum_i\rho_i\ge D-o(D).}
\tag{PB.18}

Substitution in (PB.16) gives

\[
\boxed{
 h-s_Z+2f
 \ge
 \left(1+{\pi\over4}(2-\sqrt3)-o(1)\right)D.
}
\tag{PB.19}

The new coefficient beyond the unit component-span term is

\[
{\pi\over4}(2-\sqrt3)=0.2104\ldots .
\]

---

## 8. Hostile comparison: why this does not close Type II

Equation (PB.16) is a genuinely globally injective transport statement, but (PB.19)
is **not** a new scalar contradiction. In Type II, near-isodiametric area already
forces

\[
per(F)=\pi D-o(D).
\]

The outer contact walk has length `b>=per(F)`, while (PB.15) gives

\[
h-s_Z={b+Q\over2}\ge{\pi\over2}D-o(D),
\]

whose coefficient `pi/2=1.5707...` is stronger than the `1.2104...` right side of
(PB.19), even before adding `2f`.

So the physical-boundary theorem resolves one bookkeeping problem but does not by
itself close C-II. A successful use must keep more than the final scalar collapse,
for example the angle-sensitive integrand in (PB.1), componentwise diameter data, or
a variational consequence of positive physical detour.

This also explains why simply replacing Section 120's aggregate local detour by a
globally injective physical boundary is insufficient. The source has enough ordinary
outer-boundary currency to pay the universal return constant.

---

## 9. Dependency and source-point audit

- Canonical Section 89 is used only for the elementary train facts: two physical ends
  per train and exact transverse area span.
- The global boundary classification and `b+Q=2(h-s_Z)` are canonical post-ZF face
  ledgers.
- Holes are retained through zero-host closed physical boundary walks.
- Repeated outer-boundary vertices are handled by occurrences.
- Contact-isolated source points are untouched. The theorem concerns a rhombus
  subcomplex of the nonisolated positive-contact core and never extends ZR/ZF to
  isolated coordinates.
- No KKT coefficient floor, Fox--Pach constant, Reuleaux-cap containment, Eppstein
  near-equality theorem, or source deletion is used.
- The theorem-state manifest is intentionally unchanged.

